Step 1: Determine the price of the gold jewell. We know that the price of the gold jewell is $\frac{4}{5}$ times the price of the diamond jewell. Since the price of the diamond jewell is $2000$, we can calculate the price of the gold jewell as follows:
\begin{align*}
\text{Price of gold} &= \left(\frac{4}{5}\right) \times \text{Price of diamond} \\
&= \left(\frac{4}{5}\right) \times 2000 \\
&= 1600
\end{align*}

Step 2: Calculate the price of the silver jewell. The problem states that the silver jewell costs $400 less than the price of the gold jewell. Therefore, we subtract $400 from the price of the gold jewell to get the price of the silver jewell:
\begin{align*}
\text{Price of silver} &= \text{Price of gold} - 400 \\
&= 1600 - 400 \\
&= 1200
\end{align*}

Step 3: Add up the prices of all three jewels to find the total price. We have already calculated the price of the diamond jewell ($2000), the price of the gold jewell ($1600), and the price of the silver jewell ($1200). Adding these values together gives us the total price for all three jewels:
\begin{align*}
\text{Total price} &= \text{Price of diamond} + \text{Price of gold} + \text{Price of silver} \\
&= 2000 + 1600 + 1200 \\
&= \boxed{4800}
\end{align*}
Therefore, the total price for all three jewels is $4800. @

