Step 1: Determine the initial number of orange and white fish in Bob's pond.
Bob initially has 3 orange fish and 4 white fish.

Step 2: Calculate the total number of orange and white fish before any purchases.
\begin{align*}
\text{Total orange fish} &= 3 \\
\text{Total white fish} &= 4
\end{align*}

Step 3: Find out how many orange fish are left after buying from the pet store.
The pet store sells 17 fish, which means Bob buys:
\begin{align*}
\text{Fish bought} &= 17 - 17 = 0
\end{align*}

Step 4: Use the information about the new number of orange fish to find the original number of orange fish.
Since Bob wants to have twice as many orange fish as white fish, we can set up an equation:
\begin{align*}
\text{Original number of orange fish} &= 2 \times \text{Number of white fish} \\
&= 2 \times 4 \\
&= 8
\end{align*}

Step 5: Add the remaining orange fish back into the pond.
Now, add the 8 orange fish back to the pond:
\begin{align*}
\text{New total orange fish} &= 3 + 8 \\
&= 11
\end{align*}

Step 6: Calculate the total number of fish in the pond after Bob adds the new fish.
\begin{align*}
\text{Total fish} &= 7 + 11 \\
&= 18
\end{align*}

Therefore, Bob bought 8 white fish at the pet store. The final answer is \boxed{8}.

